  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue with the persistent\n   weather pattern through Friday.\n\n-  Isolated dry thunderstorm potential: This afternoon Lemhi\n   County/SW Montana, expanding into west-central Montana on\n   Thursday afternoon.\n\n-  Elevated winds and fire weather concerns: This weekend, \n   region- wide.\n\nThis afternoon through Friday: Variable air quality remains the \nprimary short-term impact across the region as a dry, hot \nsouthwesterly flow persists over the Northern Rockies. This \npattern will hold through Friday before a Pacific trough moves \nonshore Saturday, breaking down the dominant upper-level ridge of \nhigh pressure. Historically, synoptic ridge breakdowns following \nan extended period of hot, dry weather significantly elevate fire \nweather concerns.\n\nThere will be just enough monsoonal moisture for isolated \nthunderstorms this afternoon and evening from Lemhi County into \nSouthwest Montana. This moisture pushes northward on Thursday to \ninclude areas east of US 93 and south Highway 200. Gusty outflow \nwinds of 40-50 mph will be common near any thunderstorms with a \nmarginal chance (5%) of severe gusts over 60 mph. Dry lightning \nignitions will be an additional hazard.\n\nSaturday through early next week: Winds will increase on Saturday\nas the main Pacific trough advances eastward. The strongest winds\nwill accompany and follow a dry cold-frontal passage Saturday\nafternoon into Sunday, with widespread westerly winds sustained \nat 10 to 25 mph and gusts reaching 25 to 40 mph across the region.\nThe combination of gusty winds, persistent low relative humidity,\nand dry fuels will create critical fire weather conditions, \naccordingly fire weather watches have been issued. Additionally, \nwinds will produce hazardous, choppy water on area lakes and \nrivers.\n\nThis system will remain predominantly dry with no significant \nprecipitation expected. However, post-frontal cold air advection \nwill drop temperatures 10 to 20 degrees for Sunday and Monday, \nreturning high temperatures to slightly below seasonal averages.",
